1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,287
This website doesn't know your password.

2
00:00:02,287 --> 00:00:03,567
And that's a good thing.

3
00:00:03,567 --> 00:00:05,443
When you sign up, a well-built site

4
00:00:05,443 --> 00:00:07,226
runs your password through a hash:

5
00:00:07,226 --> 00:00:10,631
a one-way function that turns it into fixed-length gibberish.

6
00:00:10,631 --> 00:00:13,663
Change one letter, and the result changes completely.

7
00:00:13,663 --> 00:00:16,300
It stores only the hash, plus a random salt,

8
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:18,466
so identical passwords look different.

9
00:00:18,466 --> 00:00:21,482
When you log in, it hashes what you typed and compares.

10
00:00:21,482 --> 00:00:24,602
Steal the database, and you get hashes, not passwords.

11
00:00:24,602 --> 00:00:27,386
Weak ones can still be guessed, one hash at a time.

12
00:00:27,386 --> 00:00:31,655
Follow: tomorrow, why 1 TB shows 931 GB.
